course: dictionary
lesson: 11291
title: Reading Roman numerals: XII
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Roman numerals are letters that stand for numbers. I means 1, V means 5 and X means 10.
explain: XII is the Roman numeral for 12. It is made of X (10) and two I's (1 + 1), so 10 + 1 + 1 = 12.
explain: «On a Roman clock I = 1, V = 5 and X = 10, so III is 3, VI is 6, IX is 9 and XII is 12.» Read the letters, then add their values.
fact: XII is the Roman numeral for 12.
fact: On a clock face with Roman numerals, XII is at the top, where 12 o'clock is.
fact: XII = X + I + I = 10 + 1 + 1 = 12.
fact: XII also numbers things such as chapters, kings and popes. In «King Charles XII of Sweden», XII tells you which Charles he was.
fact: Some clocks show 4 as IIII because clockmakers liked how it looked, so Roman numerals are not always used in exactly the same way.
exercise: Where is XII on a clock face with Roman numerals? | On the left side | *At the top | At the bottom | In the middle
exercise: What number does XII stand for? | 11 | *12 | 10 | 2
exercise: XII is made of X, I and I. What do X, I and I add up to? | 21 | 11 | 3 | *12
test: What number does VI stand for on a Roman clock? | 9 | 5 | 7 | *6
test: In «King Charles XII of Sweden», what does XII show? | The number of days he ruled | The number of soldiers | The year he was born | *The king's number: he was the twelfth Charles
exam: Which of these is the Roman numeral for 12? | VI | *XII | III | IX
